nalyzed. Two closures over the\n  1465\tsame address with different captured environments are therefore distinct\n  1466\twork items. A full pass repeats whenever it adds a work item or any dependency\n  1467\tfact, and convergence is reached only when a full pass adds neither:\n  1468\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"tail -65 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6-design/collab/1791840000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review-r3.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md; tail -45 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p5-design/collab/1791690000000-architect-srvm-phase5-route-a-review-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md; tail -25 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p5-design/collab/1791700000000-architect-srvm-phase5-route-a-review-r3.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log; sed -n '227,253p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"tail -65 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6-design/collab/1791840000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review-r3.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md; tail -45 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p5-design/collab/1791690000000-architect-srvm-phase5-route-a-review-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md; tail -25 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p5-design/collab/1791700000000-architect-srvm-phase5-route-a-review-r3.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log; sed -n '227,253p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md\"","aggregated_output":"\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-11 03:55:12 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-11 10:55:12 Asia/Bangkok\n\nThe nine previous findings are addressed. Two inconsistencies remain in the revised acceptance text. No files were edited or tests run.\n\nReferences below use `continuation.md` for `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`.\n\n| Severity | file:line | Evidence | Correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Medium — acceptance inconsistency | continuation.md:1095 | 6a item 2 still requires rows 18–22, including row 20, over all four segments. Revised row 20 must run outside the wrappers to preserve empty K; 6b correctly makes this exception. | Exclude row 20 from the four-segment group and require its single, unwrapped run separately. |\n| Low — incorrect explanation | continuation.md:1224 | The text groups a free-env cursor with a nonportable host primitive. Authentic cursor references are machine data and have an explicit carrier representation; `engine.cljc:899–905` translates them to cursor markers. The cursor operand also need not remain live after the read consumes it. | Retain the host-primitive restriction. Describe in-program cursor construction as the fixture’s chosen setup, rather than a carrier restriction. |\n\n1. **Stack lowering bases — fixed; dispute resolved in Fable’s favour for the newly specified fixture.**  \n   The expression alone contributes bases **2,4**: entering the x closure leaves `[cat [1]]`; entering y leaves `[cat [1] cat [2]]`. At the read, the current slice is `[cat [3]]`, giving stack length six. The explicit `with-cat`, `let1 s`, and `let1 c` bindings contribute three preceding empty frames. Therefore:\n   \n   - bases: **0,0,0,2,4**;\n   - saved slices: `[0,0)`, `[0,0)`, `[0,0)`, `[0,2)`, `[2,4)`;\n   - current slice: `[4,6)`.\n\n   My earlier **0,2,4** count covered `with-cat` without the two resource-binding wrappers. The revised cumulative-through-slice formula is the correct inverse, for both act and tail lowering.\n\n2. **Live-set formula — fixed.**  \n   Subtraction now occurs on virtual ids before physical mapping. For the inner call, `{1,2} − {2} = {1}`, mapping to `[0]`. Tail live-out remains empty under actual-successor liveness.\n\n3. **Free-env acquisition — fixed.**  \n   The explicit composition-owned initial map supplies the missing information without recovering it from current E. Deferring automatic retention to **4b stage 3 is acceptable**; committed stage 2 need not be amended for this design to proceed. Stage 3 must retain the checked initial map and include it in GC roots. Until then, compositions using lift must preserve and pass that map explicitly.\n\n4. **E2, X1 and integer guards — fixed.**  \n   E2 now requires lexical-key presence, including nil, and compares complete off-lexical bindings. X1 validates the root environment before frame validation. C2 guards kinds and bounds before indexing. The rule inventory correctly totals **24 rules and 25 tamper rows**.\n\n5. **Continuation-invocation oracle — fixed.**  \n   Row 15 branches on the delivered `k` before attempting another read. Invocation with `:second` re-enters the capture successor and reaches `:done` without repeating the read.\n\n6. **Refusal oracles — fixed.**  \n   Changing unshadowed y fails E2; changing lexical x does not; removing x fails presence. A non-tail replacement site produces `:site`; an otherwise valid tail with an unmatched bookkeeping key reaches `:parked-site`.\n\n7. **Placeholder renaming — fixed.**  \n   Explicit fixture correspondences replace traversal-derived assignment. Unknown markers fail the test. Substitution, canonical cell numbering and complete Merkle re-emission now support the equality claim; the unordered-container permutation gate exercises it.\n\n8. **Amendment residue — fixed.**  \n   R9 qualifies the value-profile prerequisite for engines outside the three repository lanes. U7 scopes historical body readers while preserving custody, fencing and operation-id semantics, with the carrier’s custody table governing the current representation. Export-recovery rebasing is explicitly a phase-7 prerequisite.\n\n9. **Nil fixture, harness counts and grounding — fixed except for the two findings above.**  \n   Row 22 produces `[nil \"B\"]` and keeps the nil register present across the wait. The revised matrix has **22 wrapped executable rows × four segments + one unwrapped row = 89 runs**, plus synthetic row 23.\n\n   I spot-checked more than ten anchors, including stack `716–733`, `766–772`, `838–839`; harness `69–106`; AST helpers `56–104`; module-store fixture `310–333`; analysis tests `131–139`; engine `55–60`, `312–325`, `899–905`; CBOR `842–853`; and committed 4b stage-2 evaluator `205–217`, `349–375`, `485–488`. The cursor explanation is the remaining grounding mismatch.\n\nThe amendment rulings remain:\n\n| Amendments | Ruling and target |\n|---|---|\n| F1, F4 | **ACCEPT** — frozen design |\n| F2, F3 | **ACCEPT WITH CHANGES** — frozen design; required changes are now incorporated |\n| U1, U2, U4, U5 | **ACCEPT** — UCF |\n| U3, U6 | **ACCEPT WITH CHANGES** — UCF; required changes are incorporated |\n| U7 | **ACCEPT** — UCF historical-reader scope |\n| R1, R2, R3, R4, R7, R8 | **ACCEPT** — carrier draft |\n| R5, R6, R9 | **ACCEPT WITH CHANGES** — carrier draft; required changes are incorporated |\n| R1a, R6a, R10, R11 | **ACCEPT** — carrier draft |\n| C1 | **ACCEPT** — code-as-tuples |\n\nBefore merge, align 6a’s row-20 exception with the matrix, then remove the incorrect cursor portability explanation.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED\nThe golden, allocator, liveness and descriptor corrections pass. Three specification inconsistencies remain, plus one stale count. No files edited or tests run.\n\n| Severity | file:line | evidence | correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Medium | docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md:629 | The module observable contains only `defined-keys`, but the following text promises normalization of stored values. Keys alone cannot check those values. “Every definition” also includes definitions inside closures that loading does not execute. | Compare a normalized store map alongside halting/blocking and assert its key set separately. Describe coverage as top-level execution and closure creation; retain phase-8 coverage for invoked module bodies. |\n| Medium | docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md:1308 | “The record’s input is that tree” contradicts §7.6: native derivation records have input A, while the semantic record has input tree. | Qualify this as the **semantic derivation record**; native records refer to A and reach the source tree through the semantic record/association. |\n| Medium | docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md:1092 | “Both storage addresses change” is not guaranteed. Removing a descriptor row changes checksums, but descriptors are outside the stored image payload. Payload addresses change only when payloads change. | Recompute both storage addresses from their final images without requiring inequality to previous addresses. Keep descriptor-dependent checksum regeneration separate. |\n| Low | docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md:533 | Corpus table still says six linked programs; §6.1.4 now specifies ten root programs. | Change the count to ten. |\n\n1. **H goldens — fixed; every pc recounted.** Instruction counts for rows 1–9 are respectively **8, 12, 6, 6, 12, 14, 3/9, 9, 12**. Row 7 contains two programs. The only targets are:\n   - Row 4: alternate 4, join 5.\n   - Row 5: alternate 6, join 7.\n   - Row 6: closure body 6.\n\n   All agree with the specified emitter. No old-byte comparison is required or used.\n\n2. **Actual-successor liveness and new tail golden — fixed.** Independently derived row 9:\n\n   `f→T0, g→T1, x→T2, inner-result→T3, y→T1, outer-result→T2`; peak four registers.\n\n   The inner tail call has no successors, hence `live []`. Its following layout component has `L(4)={1,2}`, which correctly does **not** determine that tail call’s live-out. The listed R image, intervals and H tail flag agree.\n\n   Two further independent derivations agree:\n   - Row 5: conditional result reuses T1; `y→T2`, outer result T3.\n   - Definition-call row: call result T3; definition result reuses T0 after operand intervals expire.\n\n3. **Lift declarations and regeneration — fixed.** Phase 8 removes both hashed lift rows, preserves version numbers under the owner ruling, and regenerates descriptor hashes and H/R checksums. Descriptor coverage survives deletion of compiler tests. The final format-keyed manifest index is coherent because A is supplied by the semantic record. Correct the storage-address assertion above.\n\n   **Hash ruling:** `jing/sha256(dc/encode-scalar descriptor)` is exactly the existing descriptor-hash computation: stack at `debruijn_code.cljc:542-548`, register at `debruijn_register_code.cljc:257-258`. It introduces no new hash use or executable identity. A continues using Jing’s default address mechanism.\n\n4. **Import/C4 gates — largely fixed.** The ten root programs and pinned outcomes match the import tests. Eight module-root runs are separately identified, and newly published install-child coverage is explicitly gated at phase 8. Correct the module observable and coverage wording above. The module fixtures’ placeholders support their expected definition-key sets; `defined-keys` remains an any-depth syntactic scanner, not an execution oracle.\n\n5. **19+1 execution rows — fixed.** Nineteen throw comparisons and the explicit zero-argument `:op/ping` bridge yielding `[true false :pong]` are consistently specified.\n\n6. **Retrievability/source association — largely fixed.** Set-valued associations, independent vector bytes, explicit carriers, no mandatory trusted-policy fetch, and the host carve-out match the rulings. Fix the derivation-record input qualification. Completeness should reject empty tree sets as well as absent entries, consistently with the non-empty rule already stated.\n\n7. **Allocator loop — fixed.** Expiry and assignment have explicit total ordering; `next` advances only for a never-used slot. Allocation no longer relies on the single-start assertion.\n\n8. **Evidence anchors — fixed.** The register linearizer reads the tail flag at line 116; my earlier 120–121 reference concerned the stack linearizer. The qualified references now distinguish them correctly.\n\n   I checked at least eight revised references: packet declarations at `import_programs.cljc:410, 473, 533, 698, 813, 890, 997, 1045, 1085`; both descriptor-hash definitions; the surviving descriptor test; and the qualified register-linearizer anchors. No new private-var access found.\n\n9. **F12 and F8–F14 — amendments substantially match the rulings.** F12 now contains the complete scanner replacement. F8/F9/F11/F12/F13’s required changes are addressed. F10’s module observable needs the correction above; F14’s migration check must allow recomputed payload addresses to remain unchanged. Settled architecture and owner choices remain intact.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED\n{\"type\":\"thread.started\",\"thread_id\":\"01a121d7-5ba4-71b1-a70b-f6b4cf3d056a\"}\n{\"type\":\"turn.started\"}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_0\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I’ll check the five text correc